  "worked_solution": "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is an exponential force. The constant factor in the exponent is $1,$ which is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $4\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-2{e}^{-\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2{e}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-\\pi /3}+2{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
